# Copy this file to .env and fill in the required values before
# `docker compose up`. See docker-compose.yml for how each is used.
#
# --- The three things every deployment actually has to do ---
# Only the connection settings have no working default at all, but these
# three are what "set this up" really means in practice - the other two
# have defaults that let the stack start, not defaults that make it playable:
#
# 1. Choose how browsers reach the server (see "Required, no sensible
#    default" below): WebRTC (NET_ICE_BROKER, recommended) or
#    WebSocket (WS_URL).
# 2. Put pak0.pak in PAK_DIR's base gamedir subfolder - id1/ by default
#    (e.g. ./paks/id1/pak0.pak), or wherever BASE_GAMEDIR points if you've
#    changed it (see "Host directory mounted read-only..." below) -
#    without this, the container starts and looks fine, but nginx has
#    nothing to serve to browsers and fteqw-server has nothing to load a
#    map with. It's mounted at /paks inside the container. GAMEDIRS adds mission packs/mods/map packs on top, GAME picks
#    which game this container runs (the one image supports them all), and BASE_GAMEDIR/SERVER_ARGS agree with that pick and (via
#    SERVER_ARGS's own "+map") which map it starts on.
# 3. Set PASSWORD to something real (see below) unless you deliberately
#    want a public, ungated server.
#
# --- One with everything ---
# Every var below, all in one place, for copy-pasting and trimming down to
# what you actually need instead of hunting through the commented-out
# defaults further down for the exact name of each one. SERVER_ARGS here
# is shown filled out as an *example*, not CloudyQuake's actual default
# (which is blank - see "Required, no sensible default" below; "-game qw"
# itself is hardcoded unconditionally, not part of SERVER_ARGS at all):
#
#   NET_ICE_BROKER=wss://quakebroker.example.com/
#   SV_HOST=quakeserver.example.com
#   PASSWORD=changeme
#   USE_LOGIN_NAME=true
#   WEB_HTTP_PORT=27501
#   SV_PORT=27500
#   SV_PORT_TCP=27500
#   PAK_DIR=./paks
#   GAME=qw
#   BASE_GAMEDIR=id1
#   GAMEDIRS=
#   SERVER_ARGS=+set hostname CloudyQuake +set deathmatch 1 -dedicated 16 +set sv_public 0 +map dm3
#   CLIENT_ARGS=+set scr_conscale 8
#   PUID=1000
#   PGID=1000
#
# Same thing, but for Quake II (GAME/BASE_GAMEDIR/SERVER_ARGS are the ones
# that actually change - see GAME below for why "-quake2" itself isn't
# part of SERVER_ARGS. CLIENT_ARGS needs nothing added for this - unlike
# Hexen II's "-hexen2", the browser client negotiates the protocol with
# the server automatically during connect):
#
#   NET_ICE_BROKER=wss://quakebroker.example.com/
#   SV_HOST=quakeserver.example.com
#   PASSWORD=changeme
#   USE_LOGIN_NAME=true
#   WEB_HTTP_PORT=27501
#   SV_PORT=27500
#   SV_PORT_TCP=27500
#   PAK_DIR=./paks
#   GAME=quake2
#   BASE_GAMEDIR=baseq2
#   GAMEDIRS=
#   SERVER_ARGS=+set hostname CloudyQuake +set deathmatch 1 -dedicated 16 +set sv_public 0 +map q2dm1
#   CLIENT_ARGS=
#   PUID=1000
#   PGID=1000

# --- Required, no sensible default ---

# How browsers reach the game server. Pick ONE of the two - see the
# README's "Deploying" section for hostnames, ports and proxy setup.
#
# Recommended - WebRTC (UDP): set NET_ICE_BROKER below.
# Alternative - WebSocket (TCP, works on any network): leave those blank and
# set WS_URL to the public wss:// URL of fteqw-server's SV_PORT_TCP, on a
# plain/unproxied DNS record with a local reverse proxy terminating TLS.
# Both are baked into config.json at container start, not build time.
#WS_URL=wss://quakeserver.example.com

# --- Optional, sensible defaults shown ---

# Shared password. Gates two independent things with the one secret:
#  - HTTP Basic Auth on the web client and pak downloads (what keeps
#    whatever you're serving - shareware, retail, a mod - from being
#    publicly downloadable, if that matters for your content).
#  - Joining the QuakeWorld server itself (fteqw's own "password" cvar).
#    This second gate exists because HTTP Basic Auth can't reach the
#    WebSocket handshake at all - browsers give JS no way to attach an
#    Authorization header to one - so the already-authenticated web client
#    is instead handed this same value via config.json and stuffs it as
#    "+password" once it connects. See nginx/auth.js's file-level comment.
#
# The username at the login prompt is never checked - players can type any
# username, and it becomes their in-game player name. Leave PASSWORD itself
# blank/unset only for a deliberately public/unlisted server with nothing
# to gate. Use a real password, not this placeholder.
PASSWORD=changeme

# Whether the login username becomes the in-game player name. Set to
# "false" to always use fteqw's own default player name instead. Combined
# with a blank PASSWORD above, setting this to "false" too skips the login
# prompt entirely - players go straight into the game.
#USE_LOGIN_NAME=true

# Host ports. Change these if they collide with something else already
# running on your homelab server.
#WEB_HTTP_PORT=27501
# SV_PORT (udp) is for native fteqw/QuakeWorld clients connecting directly,
# and can't go through any reverse proxy - see the README. SV_PORT_TCP is
# for WebSocket (browser) clients, and is what a local reverse proxy like
# nginx-proxy-manager should forward WS_URL's hostname to
# (WebSocket mode only).
# Sharing the same number for both is fteqw's own convention (they're
# independent udp/tcp sockets, so there's no actual conflict) but not
# required - change either independently if you need to.
#SV_PORT=27500
#SV_PORT_TCP=27500
# FTEMASTER_PORT is the bundled ftemaster's own listen port (the WebRTC/
# ICE broker - see NET_ICE_BROKER below and the README's WebRTC section).
# Only matters if you're actually using that.
#FTEMASTER_PORT=27950

# WebRTC (recommended), via the ftemaster broker bundled in the container -
# browsers connect over UDP instead of only WSS (TCP), avoiding TCP's
# head-of-line-blocking stalls on packet loss. Blank means WebSocket via
# WS_URL instead. See the README's "Deploying" section for the full setup
# (including fronting ftemaster with a TLS reverse proxy).
#
# NET_ICE_BROKER: setting this is what turns WebRTC on. It's the PUBLIC URL
#   browsers use to reach your ftemaster, e.g.
#   "wss://quakebroker.example.com/" through a reverse proxy (or
#   ws://localhost:27950/ for local testing). fteqw-server itself always
#   talks to the ftemaster running in the same container, so there's
#   nothing to configure for that side (FTEMASTER_HOST below overrides
#   the address it uses, if you ever need to).
# NET_ICE_NAME: optional, default /cloudyquake. This server's broker-
#   registered name - clients then "connect /cloudyquake" over
#   ICE/holepunching instead of a direct UDP/WS(S) address. Set once here,
#   used by both fteqw-server and nginx (for the browser client). It is a NAME, not a port number.
#   Maps straight to fteqw's own "sv_port_rtc" cvar
#   (RTC, not RTP - fteqw's own specs/hosting.txt itself says
#   "sv_port_rtp", but that cvar doesn't exist anywhere in the source;
#   confirmed by reading engine/common/net_wins.c directly).
#NET_ICE_BROKER=
#NET_ICE_NAME=/cloudyquake
# FTEMASTER_HOST: optional. The address fteqw-server uses to reach the
# in-container ftemaster (which doubles as its STUN server). Defaults to
# the container's own IP. Set it to a hostname that hairpins to your public
# IP if the server's STUN-reported address needs to be a public one.
#FTEMASTER_HOST=
# SV_HOST: optional hostname for the native-client command shown in
# config.json (defaults to WS_URL's hostname, else a placeholder). Must
# resolve to fteqw-server's UDP SV_PORT.
#SV_HOST=

# Host directory mounted read-only at /paks, used by both nginx (to serve
# to browsers) and fteqw-server (since - unlike CloudyDoom's
# chocolate-server - fteqw-server actually loads this data itself to run
# the game authoritatively). Laid out the same way a real install of whichever game
# you're running actually is: one subfolder per gamedir, e.g. for the
# default (stock Quake/QuakeWorld):
#
#   paks/
#     id1/pak0.pak       <- required, the base game
#     id1/pak1.pak       <- optional, full registered game instead of shareware
#     hipnotic/pak0.pak  <- an official mission pack
#     mymappack/pak0.pak <- your own map pack, mod, or anything else
#
# ...or, say, for Hexen II (BASE_GAMEDIR=data1, SERVER_ARGS=-hexen2 - see
# below), whose own retail layout calls its base gamedir data1/, not id1/:
#
#   paks/
#     data1/pak0.pak     <- required, the base game
#     data1/pak1.pak     <- optional, full registered game instead of shareware
#     portals/pak3.pak   <- the Portal of Praevus mission pack
#
# ...or for Quake II (GAME=quake2, BASE_GAMEDIR=baseq2 - see below), whose
# own retail layout calls its base gamedir baseq2/:
#
#   paks/
#     baseq2/pak0.pak    <- required, the base game
#
# The gamedir folder names (BASE_GAMEDIR, and whatever you put in GAMEDIRS
# below) and *.pak/*.pk3 filenames inside PAK_DIR are matched
# case-*insensitively* - a Windows/GOG/Steam install's "Id1/PAK0.PAK" works
# exactly the same as "id1/pak0.pak" - so you can point PAK_DIR straight at
# a copied install folder without renaming anything first.
#
# BASE_GAMEDIR is always loaded. GAMEDIRS below picks any number of
# further gamedirs to stack on top, in order - see
# init-cloudyquake-config, svc-fteqw-server and nginx/auth.js. Everything under a
# gamedir folder that isn't a *.pak/*.pk3 file is ignored by the web
# client (loose/unpacked assets aren't served to browsers), though
# fteqw-server itself will still see and use them - including a loose
# progs.dat (how Hexen II's own Portal of Praevus mission pack ships its
# improved gamecode) and loose .bsp maps, which don't need their own
# gamedir or pak at all: fteqw auto-downloads any map a client doesn't
# already have straight from the server over the game connection (see
# fteqw's own specs/browser.txt), so dropping a map into an already-loaded
# gamedir's maps/ folder just works, web and native clients alike.
#PAK_DIR=./paks

# Which game this container runs, i.e. which engine-mode flag
# svc-fteqw-server forces on at startup - see
# root/etc/s6-overlay/s6-rc.d/svc-fteqw-server/run. The gamecode for every
# supported game is baked into the one image, so this is purely a runtime
# setting: changing it needs a container restart, not a rebuild.
#
# "qw" (default): QuakeWorld, forcing "-game qw" - fteqw's own
#   openly-licensed QuakeC gamecode (quakec/basemod), compiled into the
#   image at build time. Also what Hexen II runs under (see BASE_GAMEDIR and
#   SERVER_ARGS below) - it needs no gamecode of its own baked in, since its
#   progs.dat/portals mission pack come entirely from your own retail pak
#   files, so it's just this same GAME=qw plus SERVER_ARGS=-hexen2 and
#   BASE_GAMEDIR=data1.
# "quake2": Quake II, forcing "-quake2". Unlike QuakeWorld/Hexen II (plain
#   QuakeC, interpreted by fteqw's own VM), Quake II's gamecode is a
#   natively-compiled shared library (id Tech 2's game DLL ABI) that fteqw
#   dlopen()s at runtime rather than bundling itself - built here from
#   yquake2's (github.com/yquake2/yquake2, GPLv2) src/game/ only, not its
#   client/server/renderer. Needs BASE_GAMEDIR=baseq2 below to match (the
#   baked gamecode library's own filename embeds that gamedir name). See
#   the README's Quake II section for one thing to know about your own
#   paks/baseq2/ data (player models ship as loose files, not packed).
# "quake3": Quake III Arena, forcing "-quake3". fteqw's Quake III support is
#   built into the stock engine (and the browser client), so there's no
#   gamecode to build - the game's QVMs ship in your own baseq3/pak0.pk3.
#   Needs BASE_GAMEDIR=baseq3, and e.g. SERVER_ARGS="... +map q3dm1". Don't
#   copy your install's "q3key" file into PAK_DIR. Browsers download every
#   baseq3 pk3 (~500MB with the retail pak0.pk3), so expect a long first load.
#GAME=qw

# Which PAK_DIR subfolder is fteqw's implicit default/always-loaded
# gamedir - purely CloudyQuake's own pak-volume convention, matching
# whichever game's real retail install layout you're pointing PAK_DIR at
# (see above). This has nothing to do with which fteqw flags actually run
# - that's GAME/SERVER_ARGS above/below - it just needs to agree with them:
# "id1" (the default) goes with stock Quake/QuakeWorld, "data1" goes with
# Hexen II (SERVER_ARGS=-hexen2), "baseq2" goes with Quake II (GAME=quake2),
# and so on for any other fteqw game/mod whose base gamedir isn't named id1.
#BASE_GAMEDIR=id1

# Raw, space-separated fteqw dedicated-server command line
# switches/cvars, passed straight through as fteqw's own arguments -
# reaching only fteqw-server (see CLIENT_ARGS below for the browser
# client's equivalent). Whatever fteqw itself documents for "-hexen2",
# "+set hostname", "+map", "-dedicated", etc. (see
# fte.triptohell.info/readme and specs/hosting.txt, or the in-engine
# "help" command) applies here unmodified - hostname, starting map,
# deathmatch, maxclients, sv_public, and every other ordinary session
# setting deliberately have no CloudyQuake var of their own and no default
# beyond fteqw's own: it's not this container's job to babysit settings
# that don't affect whether the container itself works, and every one
# CloudyQuake maintained separately would be one more thing that could
# drift from whatever fteqw calls it/defaults it to in some future
# version - the goal is for a new fteqw release to be a version bump here
# and nothing else, as much as possible. Blank by default - fteqw's own
# defaults apply to everything (including sv_public, which already
# defaults to not advertising - see the README). A fuller example,
# reproducing a fairly typical private-deathmatch setup by hand:
#
#   SERVER_ARGS=+set hostname MyServer +set deathmatch 1 -dedicated 16 +set sv_public 0 +map dm3
#
# Same idea for GAME=quake2 - only the map name actually needs to differ
# (id1's own map names like "dm3" don't exist in baseq2; "q2dm1" is Quake
# II's own first deathmatch map, always present in a base install):
#
#   SERVER_ARGS=+set hostname MyServer +set deathmatch 1 -dedicated 16 +set sv_public 0 +map q2dm1
#
# The engine-mode switch itself ("-game qw" or "-quake2") is always forced
# on by GAME above, not something you set (or can drop) here - Hexen II is
# the one exception, since it runs under GAME=qw's own "-game qw" and just
# needs -hexen2 added on top (fteqw's own docs describe it as FTE treating
# Hexen II as "just... a glorified mod" of the same QuakeC VM/protocol).
# Quake III is NOT achievable this way yet - fteqw does have Quake III
# support, but as a separate optional plugin with its own client-side
# cgame/ui gamecode split (unlike Quake II's server-only gamecode), which
# this project doesn't build or wire up.
#SERVER_ARGS=

# Which extra gamedirs (relative to PAK_DIR, e.g. "hipnotic" for stock
# Quake or "portals" for Hexen II) to load on top of BASE_GAMEDIR,
# space-separated, applied in order - for mission packs, QuakeC mods, or a
# map pack that ships its own gamedir (folders with nothing but loose maps
# usually don't need this at all - see PAK_DIR above). Each must exist as a
# subfolder of PAK_DIR. Leave blank for the base gamedir only. Example
# running Scourge of Armagon with a custom map pack on top: GAMEDIRS="hipnotic
# mymappack". Example running Hexen II's own mission pack:
# GAMEDIRS="portals". With GAME=quake2, "rogue"/"xatrix"/"ctf"/"action" are
# also ready to use here - their gamecode is already baked into the image,
# see the README's Quake II section - just add the matching pak data.
#GAMEDIRS=

# Raw fteqw command line flags/cvars, space-separated, given to the
# browser client only (e.g. "+set rate 25000 +set cl_nolerp 1") - the
# client-side counterpart to SERVER_ARGS above, named distinctly (not
# "EXTRA_ARGS") so it's unambiguous which side of the connection each one
# actually reaches. Unset by default - fteqw's own client defaults apply.
# See fteqw's specs/hosting.txt and the in-engine "help" command for what's
# available.
#
# If SERVER_ARGS includes an engine-mode switch like "-hexen2", add the
# same switch here too, so the browser client's own fteqw build actually
# runs it (a native client would need the same thing added to its own
# launch command/config - see the README's "Connecting" section). This is
# a QuakeC-VM-mode thing, not a protocol thing: Hexen II runs the exact
# same QuakeWorld network protocol as stock QuakeWorld, just different
# game rules/assets, so the client has to be told explicitly which rules
# to use. GAME=quake2 needs nothing added here - unlike Hexen II, Quake II
# is a genuinely different network protocol, and the client negotiates
# that with the server automatically during connect (confirmed by
# testing), so there's no equivalent "-quake2" switch to add.
# "-"-prefixed switches are parsed by fteqw independently of where they
# fall relative to any "+" commands, so it doesn't matter where in
# CLIENT_ARGS you put them.
#
# For low-vision players on modern high-resolution displays, consider:
#   CLIENT_ARGS=+set scr_conscale 8
# scr_conscale (aka vid_conautoscale) scales the HUD, console, and menu
# fonts together - the default is roughly an 8pt-equivalent size (see
# engine/client/renderer.c's own doc comment: divide your desired point
# size by 8 to get the scale value), which reads as genuinely tiny text on
# a 4K-ish display. con_textsize (default 8, a literal pixel height) scales
# just the console independently, if you want that adjusted separately.
# Both can also be typed into the in-game console (~) per-session instead
# of set here permanently.
#CLIENT_ARGS=+set scr_conscale 8

# The container's services run as this uid/gid (default 1000/1000), which
# needs to be able to read whatever's in PAK_DIR - same convention as the
# linuxserver.io images. If PAK_DIR is owned by a different uid/gid on your
# host, set both of these to match it. TZ is the usual linuxserver.io
# timezone variable. CONFIG_DIR is the host directory mounted at /config
# (fteqw's own config; logs go to the container's stdout, see `docker compose logs`).
#PUID=1000
#PGID=1000
#TZ=Etc/UTC
#CONFIG_DIR=./config

# --- Security note ---
# This compose file does not terminate TLS. HTTP Basic Auth sends
# credentials in the clear over plain HTTP, and browsers block plain ws://
# connections from an https:// page outright, so for anything beyond local
# testing this needs a TLS-terminating reverse proxy in front of it rather
# than exposing WEB_HTTP_PORT/SV_PORT_TCP directly to the internet. See the
# README for why that should be a local proxy for the game traffic
# specifically, not a remote one like Cloudflare's standard proxy.
